## Problem

The context credential rule (`SECRET/context`) treats ordinary prose and source references as `label <separator> value` assignments. The matched text becomes a plain `[REDACTED]`, even in placeholder mode. That replacement cannot be restored from the map, so placeholder mode on technical notes or design docs silently loses text and `restore(result.text, map) !== input`.

```js
import { sanitize } from '@link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer';
sanitize('The signal is weak today.').text;
// "The signal is [REDACTED]"
sanitize('The main pass is the second one.').text;
// "The main pass is [REDACTED]"
sanitize('The only downstream signal is the criterion reason code `verified_value`.').text;
// "The only downstream signal is [REDACTED]"
sanitize('| 3 | numeric guard (verification.ts:365) | mechanism PROVEN, occurrence open |').text;
// 99 characters after "verification.ts:" become [REDACTED]
sanitize('The token is valid.').text;       // unchanged (good)
sanitize('See the error code\n  `not_found`.').text;  // unchanged (good)
```

Two causes seem to combine:

1. A label prefix matches inside a longer word: `signal` is treated like `sig`, and `pass` alone is treated like `password`, also when it is a noun (`the third pass`).
2. `is` plus the rest of the sentence counts as the value, and so does `file.ts:` plus the rest of a table row.

## Expected

- Labels match whole words (or the documented affixes, such as `_key`, `-token`), so `signal`, `signals`, `passage` and `bypass` are not labels.
- `is` / `are` take a value only if it looks like a secret (a single token with entropy or shape, quoted, no spaces), not ordinary words.
- `name.ext:NUMBER` (a source path with a line number) is not an assignment.
- In placeholder mode, residual credential redactions inside free text are reported, so a caller can tell that `restore` will not be exact. For example: a count in the result, or a `restore` that throws `ERR_UNRESTORABLE` instead of returning different text.

## Acceptance

- All examples above stay unchanged, except real secrets (`password is hunter2-Xy9!q`, `api_key: sk_live_...`, which must still redact).
- No regressions in the existing credential fixtures.

## Summary

After #25 (main @ c9714cb), structured JSONL sanitization blocks almost every real AI session. 9 of 13 Claude Code and Codex session files from one project fail with `ERR_JSON` or `ERR_RESIDUAL`, and so does an ordinary public session of 23 MB. The library fails closed, so nothing leaks, but no session can be published. `ERR_JSON` is a regression from #25: the same input passed at 2306fda. `ERR_RESIDUAL` was reported in #18 and is still reproducible with a different trigger.

All source files are valid JSONL: every line parses with `JSON.parse`.

## Minimal reproductions (no personal data)

```js
const s = createSanitizer({ structured: 'jsonl' });

// 1. ERR_JSON: regression from #25, passes at 2306fda
await s.sanitizeJsonl('{"toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"\\nasync applicant is\\nasync f"}}}\n');

// 2. ERR_RESIDUAL: "state:" label followed by an escaped newline
await s.sanitizeJsonl('{"payload":{"item":{"content":[{"text":"state:\\nt"}]}}}\n');
```

The same failures occur with `sanitizeStream(source, { structured: 'jsonl', workers: 1 | 4 })` and `sanitizeStreamToFile`.

Related problems in plain-text mode:

```js
const p = createSanitizer();
await p.sanitize('{"toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"\\nasync applicant is\\nasync f"}}}');
// => {"toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"\[REDACTED] applicant is[REDACTED]"}}}
//    The escape "\n" is split: the output contains "\[" and is no longer valid JSON.
await p.sanitize('state:\nt');   // => "state:\n[REDACTED]"   ("state:" acts as a personal label for any next word)
```

## Expected

- No `ERR_JSON` for valid JSONL input. Escaped sequences (`\n`, `\t`, `\"`, `\uXXXX`) are never split by a replacement span in either mode.
- An address or state label only redacts a plausible value (letters forming a state or region name, or a postal pattern) on the same line, never a token after a newline or a single letter.
- A regression test corpus of real-shaped session records: Claude `toolUseResult.file.content`, Codex `payload.item.content[].text`, diffs and test output. Run it in CI for both `sanitizeJsonl` and streaming with 1 and 4 workers.
- Throughput from #23 could not be verified, because every tested session fails before completion.

## Summary

Add an identity-document masking transformation that keeps a short, non-identifying prefix and suffix, and replaces short values completely:

- values with 5 or more characters: first two characters + `***` + last two characters, for example `58***02`;
- values with fewer than 5 characters: complete `[REDACTED]`.

## Motivation

When publishing sanitized logs it is useful to see that a passport number or MRZ line was present and to correlate occurrences, without revealing the value. Full redaction stays the default. The existing `mask` mode has `keepStart`/`keepEnd`, and `hive-mask` keeps three characters with `…` for values longer than 12. Neither offers the "fully redact below a minimum length" rule with a fixed marker.

## Proposal

```js
createSanitizer({
  transformations: {
    PASSPORT_NUMBER: { mode: 'mask', keepStart: 2, keepEnd: 2, marker: '***', minLength: 5 },
    PASSPORT_MRZ: { mode: 'mask', keepStart: 2, keepEnd: 2, marker: '***', minLength: 5 },
  },
});
```

- `minLength` (default 5 for identity types): shorter values are fully redacted.
- `marker` replaces the hidden middle with a fixed string, so the output does not reveal the value length.
- Residual verification treats the masked output like full redaction.
- A preset such as `identityMask: true` applies this to all identity-document types.
